\startchapter[reference=conclusion, title={Conclusion and Outlook}]

% 1 sentence summary
In this thesis, I presented the developments in the field of scRNA-Seq analysis in 2016 and beyond, with a focus on my contributions to tackling arising challenges.
% intro
I introduced the behavior, identity, and differentiation of cells, and the roles that these concepts play in several complex diseases.
Furthermore, I described the insight that scRNA-Seq can grant into the processes underlying these concepts, and therefore its potential for fighting complex diseases.
I outlined the process and challenges of analyzing scRNA-Seq data in order to gain this kind of insight, with a focus on low-dimensional embeddings.
I summarized my contributions in the context of both arising challenges and improvements to the analysis process through the introduction of programmatic frameworks.

Within a few years, single-cell RNA-Seq has grown into an extensive and active field of science, with new trends and opportunities.
The largest current challenges are the ever-growing dataset sizes, and the integration of different kinds of data \cite[angerer2017,nmeth2020].
Going up to hundreds of thousands of cells, the data allows us to abandon the notion of discrete “cell types” in favor of more gradual cell identities, and explore the continuous processes at play.
Destiny \cite[angerer2016], and the gene relevance method presented in this thesis, prove to be valuable contributions in this direction.

% extensibility and openness
As demands on reliability at scale grow along with demands posed to algorithms, more practices from software engineering are finding their way into biological science.
For example, extensible and efficient data format standards are born from the collaboration of scientists to supplant the ad-hoc text-based formats we used to incompatibly modify to suit situational needs.
Open-source analysis frameworks have been designed to offer an open and welcoming environment for new as well as established analysts and method developers.
My contributions towards more \emph{interoperable}, \emph{interpretable}, \emph{reproducible}, and \emph{performant} scientific programming as described in \insec[sec:org] have helped these changes along.
\n{scanpy} and \n{AnnData} form the foundation of the \n{scverse} ecosystem and are used by many researchers in academia and in the industry \cite[virshup2023],
which will become apparent from the fact that most articles cited in this section use \n{AnnData} and integrate with \n{scanpy}.

Since the publication of the contributed articles, the field has grown substantially in scale and scope.
Experimental protocols now routinely produce data on millions of cells, and the simultaneous measurement of multiple modalities has become commonplace.
Throughout this growth, the modular approach to analysis advocated here has proven its durability:
the data infrastructure and frameworks described in this thesis remain the foundation on which new methods are built.

\startsection[title={The maturation of single-cell data infrastructure}]

The data infrastructure landscape for single-cell analysis has changed significantly over the last few years.
\n{scverse} has grown from a core of seven packages into the dominant Python ecosystem for single-cell omics.
It now comprises over 120 ecosystem packages and 11 core packages spanning data structures (\n{AnnData}, \n{MuData}, \n{SpatialData}), analysis frameworks (\n{scanpy}, \n{muon}, \n{squidpy}, \n{scvi-tools}), and domain-specific tools (\n{scirpy}, \n{SnapATAC2}, \n{rapids-singlecell}, \n{pertpy}, \n{decoupler}) \cite[virshup2023].
The nonprofit maintains a flexible governance model that manages developmental strategy, funding, community management, and planning of events, such as hackathons, workshops, and conferences.
Organizing a group of core developers mitigates the risks of having widely used scientific software that is maintained by small groups of original authors, with many of them employed on short-term academic contracts.

Single-cell analysis is however not a Python-only endeavor.
The R world is split between the \n{Bioconductor} ecosystem and \n{Seurat}.
\n{Seurat} features prominently in new research, such as the best-practices review by \inlinecite[heumos2023] and the multimodal integration benchmark by \inlinecite[liu2025].
Representatively for \n{Seurat}, the latter study notices both its high computational requirements and strong statistical performance.
\n{Seurat} evolved from a monolithic analysis tool towards a more modular framework, yet did not develop an independent data structure, consequently few independent tools built upon it.
\n{Bioconductor} has an independent data structure called \n{SingleCellExperiment}, which is used by a more healthily diverse ecosystem of tools such as \n{scater}, \n{scuttle} \cite[mccarthy2017], and \n{scDblFinder} \cite[germain2022].
\n{AnnData} bridges ecosystems through in-memory conversion with \n{anndata2ri} \cite[angerer2019b] and its on-disk representation through \n{anndataR} \cite[deconinck2025].
Both \n{Seurat} and \n{scanpy} now follow convergent best practices for preprocessing, quality control, and downstream analysis \cite[heumos2023].

% beyond technical: impact on culture
This infrastructure has had consequences beyond technical efficiency.
Standardized frameworks lower the barrier to entry for researchers new to the field as the first steps are stable, well-documented, and well-maintained.
Beyond these first steps, codification of best practices \cite[heumos2023] a product of mature infrastructure as well:
the tools had stabilized enough for consensus to emerge around parts of the workflow.
Finally reproducibility is no longer an issue: a \n{scanpy} workflow can conveniently be reproduced from a Jupyter notebook containing a manifest of Python package versions by \n{session-info2} \cite[angerer2026].

The sustainability of this infrastructure is however not guaranteed, see \insec[sec:sustainability].

\stopsection

\startsection[reference=sec:vaes, title={Dimensionality reduction and interpretability}]

Dimensionality reduction is central to the thesis at hand.
The \n{destiny} package implements both diffusion maps for nonlinear embedding of single-cell data and the gene relevance method quantifying which genes drive a given embedding.
Both address an issue that has no definitive answer to this day: ensuring interpretability of low-dimensional representations of high-dimensional data.

Since the respective articles were written, the field has engaged in a debate about what dimensionality reduction can and cannot tell us.
\inlinecite[chari2023] argued that extreme reduction from thousands of dimensions to two inevitably causes significant distortion, and demonstrated that an autoencoder could map cells into arbitrary shapes while preserving pairwise distances comparably to heuristic embeddings like UMAP or t-SNE.
They concluded that heuristic embeddings are arbitrary and should be abandoned for exploratory analysis.
The critique raised valid points: inter-cluster distances in heuristic embeddings are not meaningful, cluster sizes are artifacts of hyperparameters, and qualitative over-interpretation of visual groupings is widespread \cite[cashman2025].
Their conclusion however was controversial.
Notably absent from the discussion were diffusion maps – while the paper critiqued both linear PCA and heuristic embeddings, diffusion maps are both nonlinear and deterministic.
\inlinecite[lause2024] later showed that the metrics used by \inlinecite[chari2023] – purely distance-based – failed to capture what makes heuristic embeddings useful in practice.
Using four neighborhood and class preservation metrics, they demonstrated that these methods strongly outperform both PCA and the arbitrary-shape embedding at separating known cell identities, thereby refuting the central thesis of \inlinecite[chari2023].
Furthermore, it is possible to use evaluation metrics to quantify embedding quality, provided an adequate choice of metrics \cite[wang2025].
The outcome of this debate has been increased caution about what to read into 2D plots, yet continued use of heuristic embeddings for visualization.

This debate also reinforces the case for interpretable embeddings.
2D plots remain a central part of the analysis workflow, so quantifying their relation to the underlying data is critical.
Methods like gene relevance method address this need by quantifying gene-level contributions to the embedding.
New methods can be broadly categorized into two groups:
In addition to the gene relevance approach of post-hoc attribution to explain existing embeddings, methods have emerged that – like PCA – sport architectures that are interpretable by construction.

As examples of the first category, gradient-based attribution methods such as saliency maps and SHAP pursue the same goal as gene relevance by back-propagating through the model’s computation graph \cite[treppner2022].
The Boosting Autoencoder \cite[hackenberg2025] takes a different route, combining deep autoencoders with statistical boosting to select sparse gene sets that explain each latent dimension.

The second category consists of variational autoencoders (VAEs), which constrain the model architecture itself to produce interpretable latent spaces.
\n{DRVI} \cite[moinfar2024] uses additive decoders with nonlinear pooling to achieve disentanglement, where each latent dimension captures a distinct biological source of variation such as cell identity, developmental stage, or technical noise.
Constrained approaches like \n{VEGA} \cite[seninge2021], \n{expiMap} \cite[lotfollahi2023] and \n{OntoVAE} \cite[doncevic2023] are VAEs mapping each latent variable to a known gene program or pathway, while staying robust to incomplete knowledge.
\n{siVAE} \cite[choi2023] and \n{SIMBA} \cite[chen2024] jointly learn cell and gene embeddings, producing a gene-to-dimension loading matrix analogous to PCA loadings but with nonlinear expressiveness.

These developments reinforce the argument for interpretability made by this thesis:
dimensionality reduction does not end with a visually appealing scatter plot, but needs to be tightly integrated with a model that allows interpretation.
Especially the family of interpretable VAEs has met this challenge in a fundamentally strong way.

\stopsection

\startsection[reference=sec:trajectory, title={Multi-scale analysis and cellular heterogeneity}]

The analysis of continuous processes driving cellular heterogeneity has been enabled by fluorescent tagging and single-cell imaging.
Their targeted nature as well as their monetary and time costs however render them unsuitable for unbiased heterogeneity analysis.
While a careful selection of parameters like sequencing depth is vital, scRNA-Seq is an unbiased and inexpensive method able to detect rare cells \cite[torre2018].
This makes it uniquely suited for heterogeneity analyses.
A rarely addressed property of cellular heterogeneity is its multi-scale nature:
multiple processes at different scales and localities shape the heterogeneity of either subpopulations or all cells (see \insec[sec:visualization]).

Analysis algorithms in this area have evolved alongside the protocols able to sequence hundreds of thousands of cells.
Suitable for the low sample sizes of bulk RNA-Seq, simple linear dimension reduction methods like PCA were used to visualize early single-cell studies.
Nonlinear methods like diffusion maps, t-SNE or later UMAP have been developed and continue to be improved to fit the nature of transcriptome data.
But while they capture both continuity and heterogeneity at multiple scales, their output does not contain directly interpretable information about processes at different scales.
Methods like the growing multitude of trajectory inference algorithms or gene relevance complement them within the same constraint \cite[saelens2019].

Since \n{DPT} was incorporated into \n{destiny} and \n{scanpy}, trajectory inference has matured substantially.
\n{PAGA} \cite[wolf2019] infers hierarchical cell lineage structure from RNA-Seq data.
\n{CellRank} \cite[lange2022] computes directed, probabilistic fate maps using RNA velocity while \n{CellRank\,2} \cite[weiler2024] generalizes this to multimodal data.
It stood at the beginning of a trend of combining optimal transport with multi-modal data – especially spatial – for trajectory inference, continued by several other methods.
These include \n{STORIES} \cite[huizing2026], which extends optimal transport to infer differentiation potentials from spatial transcriptomic data.
Optimal transport also allows to match cell measurements from different feature spaces, as done by \n{GENOT} \cite[klein2024], which combines optimal transport with flow matching to this end.
\n{moscot} \cite[klein2025b] is a toolbox to apply scalable optimal transport to multi-modal time-series data in order to infer trajectories, match cells, and model spatiotemporal dynamics in other ways.

% A complementary development is the metacell concept.
% \n{SEACells} \cite[persad2023] and \n{MetaCell-2} \cite[ben-kiki2022] group highly similar cells into compact units that overcome the sparsity of individual measurements while retaining biological heterogeneity.
% This enables genuinely multi-scale analysis: metacells provide a coarser resolution that reveals global structure, while the constituent single cells remain available for fine-grained investigation.
% The metacell abstraction is particularly useful for trajectory inference and cell-type deconvolution at scale.

Analysis of multi-modal data (see \insec[sec:multimodal]) promises advances in understanding of developmental heterogeneity, an important part of multi-scale heterogeneity.
So far, few approaches have attempted to capitalize on the large datasets that are being compiled as part of cell atlas initiatives.
\n{PULSAR} \cite[pang2025] is one of the first methods to directly address the multi-scale challenge this way: it is a foundation model (see \insec[sec:transformers]) designed to learn representations at multiple biological scales, from gene programs to multicellular systems, and to relate them to each other.
The question of how individual genes drive heterogeneity at different scales remains open, but the field is getting close:
some of the interpretable VAE approaches discussed in \insec[sec:vaes] go down to the individual gene level without disentangling multiple scales, while \n{PULSAR} offers interpretability at the gene program level combined with multi-scale analysis.

\stopsection

\startsection[reference=sec:transformers, title={Transformers and the vision of the virtual cell}]

Perhaps the most visible development in computational biology in recent years is the application of generative models to single-cell data.
An early example is \n{scGen} \cite[lotfollahi2019], which used a VAE for perturbation prediction by learning a latent representation of cellular identity and perturbation direction (see \insec[sec:vaes]).
Since then, especially transformer architectures have seen a steep rise in adoption.
Originally developed for natural language processing, transformers have been adapted to learn representations from large collections of gene expression profiles like cell atlases.

% first wave
A first wave of single-cell transformers was introduced in 2023–2024, aiming to produce foundation models pre-trained on tens of millions of cells that can be fine-tuned for specific tasks.
\n{scGPT} \cite[cui2024] proposed to address cell typing, perturbation prediction, batch correction, and multi-omics integration,
while \n{Geneformer} \cite[theodoris2023] encoded the multi-scale regulation of gene regulatory network inference.
These models attracted significant attention, yet \inlinecite[szalata2024] identified several shortcomings in performance and approach, such as transcriptomes fundamentally lacking an order expected by this language model derived approach.
Later, benchmarks have shown even more clearly that this approach rarely outperforms simpler approaches, e.g. and on perturbation prediction specifically, even linear models can be competitive \cite[ahlmann-eltze2025].
While transformer models contain autoencoders, their representations are opaque unlike the interpretable VAEs discussed in \insec[sec:vaes]:
latent dimensions do not map to identifiable biological processes, which limits the models’ utility for hypothesis generation and mechanistic understanding.

% second wave
A more targeted second wave of methods has begun to address these shortcomings.
\n{State} \cite[adduri2025] focuses specifically on perturbation prediction by training on more than a hundred million of perturbed cells, resulting in a markedly improved performance.
\n{Stack} \cite[dong2026] builds on the foundation model idea, but allows in-context learning, which enables predictions about unseen conditions without fine-tuning.
Notably, \n{Stack}’s tokenization into gene module vectors provides some degree of interpretability to its latent space.
% both flow matching, include?
\n{scLDM} \cite[palla2025] forges the order-dependency of transformer architectures by using latent diffusion models in oder to generate realistic expression profiles for use cases like virtual experiments.
\n{CellFlow} \cite[klein2025a] uses flow matching to model single-cell phenotypic responses to complex perturbations, including combinatorial treatments.

% interpretability as core weakness
Interpretability remains a central limitation of these models, with not even \n{Stack}’s gene-module tokens going down to the single gene level.
Especially for medical applications, the lack of direct mechanistical reasoning about the underlying biology of a prediction might be a roadblock.

% beyond technical: virtual cell vision
The concept motivating the approach of generalist “foundation models” is the vision of the “virtual cell”: an model that can simulate cellular behavior in-silico, predicting how cells respond to perturbations, drugs, and disease \cite[bunne2024].
Taking a step back from the single-cell level, \inlinecite[shmatko2025] demonstrate that incomplete knowledge can lead to useful predictions with \n{Delphi-2M}, a GPT-style model trained on the patient level to predicting disease trajectories up to 20 years into the future.
The vision of the virtual cell, if realized, could accelerate drug discovery, enable personalized treatment planning at a fraction of cost it has today, and reduce the need for animal experiments.
Without interpretability however, even a perfect black box of a virtual cell would only help with speed and cost, but but not with insight.

% TODO: large scale as enabler

\stopsection

\startsection[reference=sec:multimodal, title={Multimodal integration and spatial transcriptomics}]

Recent developments in the joint measurement of scRNA-Seq data and multiple other data types at single-cell resolution have enabled multimodal analysis.
Nature Methods named single-cell multimodal omics its method of the year 2019 \cite[nmeth2020], and spatially resolved transcriptomics its method of the year 2020 \cite[nmeth2021spatial].
\inlinecite[efremova2020] provided an early review of the different single-cell modes that became jointly measurable with scRNA-Seq in short succession: RNA velocity, spatial location, surface protein abundances, epigenetic modifications, and chromatin accessibility.

% spatial explosion
Of these, spatial transcriptomics has seen the most dramatic growth.
In recent years, the field has expanded to encompass microarray-based, bead-based, and nanoball approaches at resolutions from tissue-level to subcellular \cite[moses2022].
\n{SpatialData} \cite[marconato2024] and \n{squidpy} \cite[palla2022] provide \n{AnnData}-based infrastructure for this modality within \n{scverse},
while new models show the promise of this modality, be it trajectory inference methods (see \insec[sec:trajectory]) or spatial prediction models like \n{Nicheformer} \cite[tejada-lapuerta2025].

% multimodal infrastructure
The multimodal infrastructure has matured as anticipated in the contributed articles.
\n{MuData} and \n{muon} \cite[bredikhin2022] fill the role of storing and jointly analyzing multiple modalities for common sets of observations.
\n{SnapATAC2} \cite[fang2021,zhang2024], built on \n{AnnData}, has become a core \n{scverse} package for chromatin accessibility analysis.
Surface protein measurements via CITE-Seq \cite[gayoso2019] and epigenetic profiling via scCUT\&Tag \cite[kaya-okur2019] are now routinely combined with transcriptomic data.
Parts of the multimodal landscape are beginning to settle, for example multimodal integration methods have been systematically benchmarked by \inlinecite[liu2025], leading to guidance based on the choice of which downstream task to optimize for.
% they evaluated dimension reduction, batch correction, cell typing, imputation, feature selection and spatial registration across many method-dataset combinations.
Additionally, the need for integration across modalities is diminishing as protocols emerge that measure multiple modalities simultaneously, such as SUM-seq \cite[lobato-moreno2025] for combined chromatin accessibility and gene expression profiling from millions of cells.

A cell’s identity is not fully captured by its transcriptome alone; while an useful proxy, it is unlikely that we achieve insight on a level coming close to the “virtual cell” (see \insec[sec:transformers]) without integrating across modalities.
From the perspective of this thesis, this means a need for robust data structures encapsulating the relationships between modalities and fast frameworks able to handle their combinatorial explosion.

\stopsection

\startsection[title={From cell census to biological insight}]

The developments described above are not purely methodological; they demonstrate a shift in how single-cell data can help us gain insight into healthy development, homeostasis, and disease.

The \n{Human Cell Atlas} has progressed from a white paper \cite[regev2017] to the point where a first draft is being assembled that will encompass around 100 million cells across all major organ systems \cite[rood2025].
It will among others contain integrated atlases of the lung, gut, organoid, and nervous system.
\inlinecite[lotfollahi2024] draw an analogy to reference genomes: they predict that similar to the established practice of quantifying transcriptomes by mapping reads to a reference genome, mapping new single-cell data to curated reference atlases could replace manual, unsupervised clustering and annotation.
Reference mapping workflows project query cells into an atlas-defined coordinate space, enabling automated cell identity annotation, detection of novel populations absent from the reference, and prediction of unmeasured modalities such as spatial location or chromatin accessibility.
Foundation models are taking steps towards this vision as described in \insec[sec:trajectory] \andsec[sec:transformers].
Cell atlases like the \n{Human Cell Atlas} are therefore approaching their vision of becoming active infrastructure for computational modeling \cite[rood2025], much as reference genomes became infrastructure for genomics and transcriptomics.

Single-cell analysis is also beginning to influence pharmaceutical practice.
During the COVID-19 pandemic, single-cell technologies were used to measure B cell receptor repertoires to characterize immune responses, and to develop neutralizing antibodies.
Beyond infectious diseases, \inlinecite[vandesande2023] review applications of scRNA-Seq in drug discovery, including target discovery at cell identity level, high-throughput perturbation screening, and insight into different aspects of disease such as cancer metastasis,Alzheimer’s and auto-immune diseases.

The growing ability to predict cellular responses to perturbations in-silico \cite[adduri2025,klein2025a,dong2026] could further accelerate this path towards clinical insight.
Virtual perturbation screens are realistically able to substantially narrow the space of promising drug candidates before wet-lab validation, driving down the cost and time of drug development, which in turn might push several orphan drugs back into the realm of financial feasibility.
\n{Scanpy} and \n{AnnData} already play an important role in this effort by forming the base of many pharmaceutical researchers’ daily work.

\stopsection

\startsection[reference=sec:sustainability, title={Sustaining open scientific software}]

\n{Scanpy} has proven to be scalable in terms of CPU and memory through its efficient implementation, multiprocessing capabilities and \n{AnnData}’s out-of-core mode,
which continue to be actively developed for both projects.
For a successful open-source project, more is necessary than just performance and ease of use.
Open source projects need to be scalable in terms of maintenance structure and trust \cite[delaat2010].

The \n{scverse} project has made concrete progress in this direction since its inception.
Its code of conduct (CoC), governance model, and formalized inclusion criteria for ecosystem packages distributes decision-making beyond the original authors.
CoCs have been shown to increase diversity in a project, while higher diversity is linked to increased productivity \cite[tourani2017].

There is a fundamental misalignment between academic incentives rewarding novelty and the need for long-term software maintenance \cite[strasser2022].
\inlinecite[coelho2024] observes that most research software is built by trainees on short-term grants, leading to abandoned tools after publication, and argues for a minimum five-year commitment.
At the same time open-source scientific software relies on grants and other goal-driven fund distribution models rather than long-term employment by neutral entities like nation states.
The result is a landscape where widely used packages depend on the continued voluntary effort of a small number of individuals, many of whom hold temporary academic positions.

The \n{scverse} governance model tries to combat this problem by decoupling software maintenance from the interests of individual labs and people:
to this end it includes an open inter-lab management committee, and formalized criteria for both community conduct and inclusion of new ecosystem packages.
We hope to be able to reduce reliance on any single contributor by encouraging contributions from junior developers and data scientists, distributing key knowledge across more people, and relying on structured development practices and automation.

In the end, no matter how brilliant it is what we build, there have never been giants on whose shoulders to stand, only scaffolding that has to be maintained.

\stopsection

